he paper's relevant method is the SELFIES-based pretraining of the MOLGEN molecular language model. It converts 100 million unlabeled ZINC-15 molecules into SELFIES strings, randomly replaces tokens with a mask token, encodes the corrupted SELFIES with a bidirectional BART encoder, and decodes the original SELFIES left-to-right using an autoregressive decoder. The training objective is cross-entropy reconstruction between the decoder output and the original SELFIES sequence. A second stage adds domain-agnostic prefix tuning by prepending shared tunable prefix vectors to the keys and values of multi-head attention at each layer and updating the full model on 2.22 million molecules from synthetic and natural-product domains. This relates to the proposed research method because both use SELFIES as the molecular representation for language-model training, but the paper does not use GDB-13, complete molecular subsets, OPT 1.3B, recall prediction, or explicit equivalence-class evaluation.", 'results': 'The supplied excerpt does not report quantitative experimental outcomes for the SELFIES language-modeling method; the MAIN RESULTS section is empty. The only reported numerical observation is representation-level: after a random single mutation, SMILES retains validity with 9.9% likelihood, whereas SELFIES retains validity with 100% likelihood. No precision, recall, generation counts, baseline comparisons, or statistical significance for molecular generation are provided in the excerpt.', 'context': 'Experimental conditions include a 100 million molecule pretraining corpus from ZINC-15 filtered to reactive, purchasable molecules with molecular weight at most 500 Daltons and LogP at most 5, and a 2.22 million molecule second-stage corpus spanning synthetic and natural-product domains. The SELFIES vocabulary size is 185. The model is described as BART, but parameter count, hardware, training steps, learning rate, batch size, and sampling settings are not provided in the excerpt. Downstream evaluation is described as covering molecular distribution learning, targeted molecule discovery, constrained optimization, and property optimization, but the provided text does not include the corresponding metrics or results. This context matters for the research plan because it shows SELFIES can support large-scale molecular language modeling, while leaving open the recall-specific benchmarking and equivalence-class handling proposed in the plan.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2985, 'output_tokens': 2850}, {'corpus_id': '235411073', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '235368071', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '268788258', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1595, 'output_tokens': 5779}, {'corpus_id': '237452253', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper studies molecule description generation as an image captioning task. It constructs MOLCAP from 81,230,291 unique canonical SMILES, samples 1,000,000 molecules for training and 5,000 for evaluation, renders each molecule as a 256x256 image using RDKit, and tokenizes captions using character, SELFIES, and BPE tokenizers with vocabulary sizes 100, 200, 500, 2000, and 20000. The IMG2SMI model uses an encoder-decoder architecture, with a transformer encoder/decoder configuration compared against an RNN plus attention decoder, and with the image feature extractor either fixed or fine-tuned. Evaluation compares generated captions to references using ROUGE and BLEU on a 200-vocabulary tokenization, Levenshtein distance, exact match after canonicalization, captioning percentage, molecular validity, and Tanimoto similarity computed from RDK, Morgan, and MACCS fingerprints. Baselines include OSRA at 70 percent confidence, a pretrained DECIMER model, and a random all-pairs baseline on 5,000 molecules. This is related to the proposed method because it trains on a molecular subset and explicitly compares SELFIES with SMILES and BPE tokenizations, and it uses canonical exact match as a proxy for molecular equivalence, although it does not train a standalone language model on GDB-13 or measure recall over equivalence classes.', 'results': 'IMG2SMI outperformed OSRA by more than 163 percent on MACCS fingerprint Tanimoto similarity and achieved almost a 10-fold improvement on ROUGE. Exact match remained below 10 percent, BLEU improved by only 17 percent, and Levenshtein distance was 21, with average generated caption length 47.66 characters versus 43.94 for references. Existing systems performed only slightly better than the random molecule baseline, while IMG2SMI exceeded the 0.85 Tanimoto similarity threshold. In tokenization ablations, the paper reports that few captions from non-SELFIES tokenizers were valid molecules, and the authors recommend SELFIES; if BPE was used, vocabulary size 2000 was best on average. Transformer decoding outperformed RNN plus attention, which only slightly beat random; a fixed encoder with RNN fell below random, while a fixed encoder with transformer still outperformed existing methods, and fine-tuning the encoder improved all models. Beam search did not improve performance and larger beams decreased performance. No statistical significance tests were reported.', 'context': "The dataset was limited to a 1,000,000-molecule image subset because each 1,000,000-molecule image set was about 200 GB and the full MOLCAP image set would have been about 16.24 TB. The evaluation set was 5,000 molecules because larger samples did not increase sensitivity and were slower, matching common image captioning validation sizes. Images were 256x256 RDKit renderings, and the authors did not study image size or drawing-program variation. Comparisons across SELFIES, SMILES, and BPE variants were made after translating outputs to SMILES. Hardware, training steps, learning rates, and other hyperparameters were not reported. The paper's setting is image-conditioned molecule captioning rather than recall-oriented language-model generation, so it provides supporting evidence for SELFIES validity and canonical equivalence evaluation but not direct evidence for the proposed GDB-13 recall benchmark or OPT-based recall prediction."}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 4292, 'output_tokens': 4091}, {'corpus_id': '212415210', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper evaluates SELFIES as a molecular string representation for generative modeling. It first performs a robustness test by taking a valid molecule string (MDMA), introducing one, two, or three random symbol mutations, and checking validity with RDKit for SMILES, DeepSMILES, and SELFIES. It then trains standard variational autoencoders and generative adversarial networks on the QM9 small-molecule benchmark using both SMILES and SELFIES tokenizations. For the VAE, the authors sample points from randomly oriented planes through the latent space and displaced planes, decode them, and measure the fraction of valid molecules. For the GAN, they train 200 hyperparameter settings for each representation, sample 10,000 molecules from each trained generator, and count unique valid molecules to assess diversity. This relates to the proposed method because the plan trains molecular language models on SELFIES strings and compares SELFIES to SMILES; the paper provides evidence that SELFIES improves validity and diversity in molecular generative models, though it does not use autoregressive language models, GDB-13, or explicit equivalence-class computation.', 'results': 'For random mutations from MDMA, SMILES validity was 26.6% for one mutation, 9.0% for two, and 3.7% for three; DeepSMILES was 58.9%, 35.8%, and 24.3%; SELFIES was 100% valid in all tested cases. In VAE latent-space sampling, SMILES produced only a small fraction of valid molecules, whereas SELFIES produced valid molecules across the sampled latent-space planes. The SELFIES VAE latent space was reported to be more than two orders of magnitude denser in unique molecules than the SMILES VAE when sampling stopped after 20 consecutive samples yielded no new molecule. For GANs, the best SELFIES configuration produced 78.9% diverse molecules, while the best SMILES configuration produced 18.6% diverse molecules. No statistical significance tests are reported.', 'context': 'Experiments used the QM9 benchmark dataset of small organic molecules. Validity was assessed with RDKit. The VAE latent space is described in the excerpt as having 241 dimensions, likely a typographical rendering of a smaller latent dimensionality, and validity was evaluated on randomly oriented planes through the origin and planes displaced by (-2, -1, +1, +2) standard deviations. GAN experiments used 200 hyperparameter settings per representation and 10,000 sampled generations per model. The paper does not specify hardware, exact optimizer settings, token vocabulary sizes, or training epochs, and it does not evaluate recall, canonical equivalence classes, or GDB-13 molecular subsets.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 3061, 'output_tokens': 3520}, {'corpus_id': '249463675', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1382, 'output_tokens': 338}, {'corpus_id': '76665438', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '964287', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2167, 'output_tokens': 752}, {'corpus_id': '237648365', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '3347345', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1423, 'output_tokens': 691}, {'corpus_id': '53223613', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text availa